{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_bqlkt3rugd245g6dbe4hamkkb4ynum3jbdlltholveaaw2uwhllq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the C code — `ast_lift` requires that the LAST node and its parent are both closed. My test didn't close the outer. Let me look at how it's actually used:\n\nActually, `ast_lift` looks at the last 4 par entries: it should be `[outer_open, inner_open, inner_close, outer_close]`. So we DO need to close outer first. But that creates a different scenario. Let me re-read the C code more carefully:\n\nLooking at C:\n```c\nc1 = ast->par_cnt-1;       // last (must be close)\nc2 = c1-1;                  // (must be close)\no1 = c1+ast->par[c1];       // open of c1\no2 = c2+ast->par[c2];       // open of c2\nif (o2 != o1+1) return;     // o2 must be exactly inside o1\n```\n\nSo o1 is the OUTER open, o2 is the INNER open. We need outer_open=o1, inner_open=o2=o1+1, inner_close=c2, outer_close=c1. That means we need:\n- open outer (creates par[0]=outer)\n- open inner (creates par[1]=inner)\n- close inner (creates par[2]=-1) \n- close outer (creates par[3]=-3)\n\nAfter lift: outer is removed, leaving only `[inner, -1]`. Let me fix the test:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2999,"cache_read_input_tokens":115618,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2999,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":48,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13b6b938-36e3-41b2-aba1-97dd48cdad01","uuid":"78443a8a-c025-4c8a-8b48-b9d65d849057"}
